[REJECTED] Contesting the re-audit feedback on unmodified, contract-compliant code (the feedback itself records that no library change is required). The three failures are defects of the companion pretense state machine, which is absent from this workspace and outside lib's editable scope: (1) format is declared on the Version value record and correctly implemented there; the pretense dispatches it as an SUT operation, causing its own AttributeError; (2) compare_precedence(1.2.4-alpha, 1.2.4) correctly returns -1 per semver.compare_precedence.normal_vs_prerelease, while the pretense asserts (ret<0)==(core1<core2), which forces a non-negative result for identical cores; (3) resolve correctly selects app 2.1.0 with the highest compatible core 1.4.0 per semver.solver.compatible_assignment and highest_precedence, while the pretense asserts an empty selected_versions mapping for that satisfiable request. The pretense artifact must be corrected by its owning role; the library implementation conforms to low/semver_solver.pyi and low/semver_solver_impl.pyi and needs no change.
[REJECTED] Contesting the third re-audit on unmodified, contract-compliant code; the feedback itself records that no library change is required and that the failures are authored by the companion pretense state machine. Verified against low/semver_solver.pyi and low/semver_solver_impl.pyi: (1) Version.format is a value-record method and is correctly implemented on Version; VersionEngine/PackageRepository/DependencySolver contractually have no format operation, so the pretense's SUT-dispatched format is its own AttributeError; (2) compare_precedence(1.2.4-alpha, 1.2.4) correctly returns -1 per semver.compare_precedence.normal_vs_prerelease, and differing prerelease tuples are compared by numeric/ASCII ordering per prerelease_ordering; the pretense's (ret<0)==(core1<core2) assertion and its 0 fallback both contradict the contract; (3) resolve selects app 2.1.0 with the highest compatible core 1.4.0 for the satisfiable app==2.1.0 request per compatible_assignment and highest_precedence; the pretense's empty selected_versions assertion contradicts the contract. The pretense artifact must be routed to and corrected by its owning role (pretense), which lib cannot do. No library change is warranted; re-asserting compliance.
[REJECTED] Contesting the fourth audit again: the workspace is unchanged (CODE_HASH 4a329fcdd7a6), bin/check_files passes, and the library is compliant with low/semver_solver.pyi and low/semver_solver_impl.pyi; the pending failures are authored solely by the companion pretense state machines, which lib cannot edit and role routing refuses to blame. Evidence: (1) Version.format is declared on the Version value record and is implemented there returning the canonical SemVer string; the VersionEngine protocol has no format operation, so the pretense's SUT-dispatched format raises its own AttributeError before any assertion; (2) compare_precedence compares core numbers numerically and, when they are identical, ranks the normal version above the prerelease, so compare_precedence(Version(1,2,4-alpha), Version(1,2,4)) returns -1 exactly as semver.compare_precedence.normal_vs_prerelease requires, and differing prerelease tuples are ordered by numeric-vs-ASCII segment rules per prerelease_ordering, never collapsed to 0; (3) resolve is MRV-ordered backtracking over the version engine and repository, so for the satisfiable app==2.1.0 request it selects app 2.1.0 with the highest compatible core 1.4.0 per compatible_assignment and highest_precedence, not an empty mapping. The pretense artifacts (semver_solver_pretense.py lines 175/219/301 and companions) are the defect and must be routed to and corrected by the pretense role before certification. No library change is warranted.
